//! The ordinary names for the sealed thread properties `Transferable` and `Concurrent`.
//!
//! # When to use
//! Bound a type parameter on [`Transferable`] when a value of it moves to another thread, and on
//! [`Concurrent`] when several threads use one shared borrow of it at once. Write
//! `impl Transferable for T {}` or `impl Concurrent for T {}` to assert a property for a type whose
//! stored parts cannot prove it, such as a type that owns storage through a raw pointer.
//!
//! # Details
//! Both names are transparent `type` aliases (TYPE-004) of the compiler-sealed properties
//! `Intrinsic.Transferable` and `Intrinsic.Concurrent` (THREAD-001). The compiler derives both
//! from a type's stored parts (THREAD-002): scalars, text and enums have both, a raw pointer and
//! `Shared<T>` have neither, `&T` needs a `Concurrent` referent, and a record, union, array or
//! closure has a property when every part it stores does.
//!
//! # Gotchas
//! An `impl` of either property is an unchecked assertion (THREAD-003): the compiler does not look
//! inside the type, so the declaring module is responsible for synchronizing the storage the type
//! reaches. The `impl` declares no members.
//!
//! # Examples
//! ## Require a value that may cross threads
//! ```silk
//! import silk.thread { Transferable }
//!
//! struct Job { id: i32 }
//!
//! fn handOff<T: Transferable>(value: T) -> T {
//!   return move value
//! }
//!
//! pub fn main() -> i32 {
//!   let job = handOff(Job { id: 42 })
//!   return job.id
//! }
//! ```

/// The sealed property of types whose values may move to another thread.
pub type Transferable = Intrinsic.Transferable

/// The sealed property of types whose shared borrows several threads may use at once.
pub type Concurrent = Intrinsic.Concurrent
